==English==

===Etymology===
From a monologue delivered by the character [[w:Polonius|Polonius]] in Act I Scene III of ''[[w:Hamlet|Hamlet]]'' by [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]].

===Proverb===
{{head|en|proverb|head=[[to]] [[thine]] [[own]] [[self]] [[be]] [[true]]}}

# [[be oneself|Be yourself]]; be [[true]] to yourself; do not engage in [[self-deception]].
#* '''c. 1599-1601''', William Shakespeare, ''[http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Tragedy_of_Hamlet,_Prince_of_Denmark Hamlet]'', Act I, Scene III:
#*: This above all: '''to thine ownself be true''',
#*: And it must follow, as the night the day,
#*: Thou canst not then be false to any man.
#* '''1977''', ''The Psychological and Social Impact of Physical Disability'' (eds. Robert P Marinelli and Arthur E. Dell Orto), Springer Publishing Co. (1977), ISBN 9780826122100, page 306:
#*: "'''To thine own self be true'''," I saw, was what produced vitality, confidence, and genuine expression in one's interpersonal relations.
#* '''1986''', Gary Diedrichs, "[http://books.google.com/books?id=jxIEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA27 Bewitched]", ''Orange Coast'', August 1986:
#*: Know thyself. '''To thine own self be true'''. For the man or woman who can confront the demon within, there is a hopeful prognosis.
#* '''1995''', [[w:Paula Rodriguez Rust|Paula C. Rust]], ''Bisexuality and the Challenge to Lesbian Politics: Sex, Loyalty, and Revolution'', New York University Press (1995), ISBN 081477444X, [http://books.google.com/books?id=U7qt-VKvDc8C&pg=PA51 page 51]:
#*: Several of these women said simply, "to each her own," while others like Sue were only slighty more verbose: "Each of us has a right  and a responsibility ''''to thine own self be true'''.' Another person's sexual preference is not my business or concern."
#* '''2004''', James M. Morris & Andrea L. Kross, ''Historical Dictionary of Utopianism'', Scarecrow Press (2004), ISBN 0810849127, [http://books.google.com/books?id=odgOl06HasQC&pg=PA262 page 262]:
#*: "'''To thine own self be true'''" whatever the consequences was taken as the principle of true freedom and humanity by the romantics.
#* '''2012''', Mark D. White, "The Sound and the Fury Behind 'One More Day'", in ''Spider-Man and Philosophy: The Web of Inquiry'' (ed. Jonathan J. Sanford), John Wiley & Sons (2012), ISBN 9780470575604, [http://books.google.com/books?id=oEHbCwcV-NEC&pg=PA241 page 241]:
#*: As Shakespeare wrote, "'''To thine own self be true'''," at least according to what kind of person you believe yourself to be.

